### Definition: The Formal Contract

The official Java specification defines the **Contract** between `equals()` and `hashCode()` as the **Golden Rule** of object equivalence:

> **The Golden Rule:**
> If two objects are equal according to the `equals(Object)` method, then calling `hashCode()` on each of the two objects **must produce the exact same integer result**.

$$\text{If } a.\text{equals}(b) == \text{true} \implies a.\text{hashCode}() == b.\text{hashCode}()$$

**The Converse is NOT Required:**
If two objects have the same hash code, they do *not* have to be equal (this is simply a hash collision). However, equal objects must always produce identical hash codes.

---

### Demonstrating Collection Failure When Violating the Contract

```java
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

// BROKEN: Overrides equals() but FAILS to override hashCode()
class BrokenKey {
    private final String id;
    public BrokenKey(String id) { this.id = id; }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object obj) {
        if (this == obj) return true;
        if (obj == null || getClass() != obj.getClass()) return false;
        return java.util.Objects.equals(id, ((BrokenKey) obj).id);
    }
    // Missing hashCode() override! Uses default System memory address!
}

public class ContractViolationDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Set<BrokenKey> keySet = new HashSet<>();
        BrokenKey k1 = new BrokenKey("API-USER-99");
        keySet.add(k1);

        BrokenKey k2 = new BrokenKey("API-USER-99"); // Logically equal to k1!

        System.out.println("k1.equals(k2): " + k1.equals(k2)); // true!
        System.out.println("keySet.contains(k2): " + keySet.contains(k2)); // FALSE! Contract broken!
    }
}
```

**Console Output:**
```text
k1.equals(k2): true
keySet.contains(k2): false
```

---

### Why the Item Was Not Found

`HashSet` checked `k2.hashCode()`, calculated a completely different bucket index than `k1`, and never even called `equals()`. Always override both methods simultaneously using the exact same set of fields.

